                <pb facs="0988" n="976" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg976"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">550.41B1/56: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-02-01T19:00:00+01:00">February 1, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 1—6:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>40. Department’s circular of January 29, 3 p.m. During my call at the Foreign
                    Office this afternoon I expressed the hope that the Italian Government would be
                    represented at the Technical Textile Conference. In reply Count Ciano<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d970-f1"/> informed me that inasmuch as the Italian Government
                    was no longer represented on the League or on the International Labor Conference
                    he was not in a position to send a delegate to the Textile Conference. On the
                    other hand, if before April 2, Italy should again become associated with the
                    League and the labor organization, Italy would be represented at the
                    Conference.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d970-f1" n="5" xml:id="d970-f1-content">
                <p>Italian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
